  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "id": "gesundheit-body",
      "title": "Body parts and their plurals",
      "body": "Most body parts are **der** words. The ones to watch are **die Hand**, **das Bein** and **das Auge**.\n\nIn the plural the article is always **die**. Many short *der* words add an umlaut and **-e**: *der Fuß → die Füße*. **Rücken** stays the same, and **Auge** adds **-n**.\n\nLearn each body part with its article and plural.",
      "table": [
        ["Singular", "Plural", "English"],
        ["der Kopf", "die Köpfe", "head"],
        ["der Hals", "die Hälse", "neck, throat"],
        ["der Rücken", "die Rücken", "back"],
        ["der Bauch", "die Bäuche", "stomach"],
        ["der Arm", "die Arme", "arm"],
        ["die Hand", "die Hände", "hand"],
        ["das Bein", "die Beine", "leg"],
        ["der Fuß", "die Füße", "foot"],
        ["das Auge", "die Augen", "eye"],
        ["der Zahn", "die Zähne", "tooth"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gesundheit-wehtun",
      "title": "wehtun: saying what hurts",
      "body": "**wehtun** (to hurt) is a separable verb, like *aufstehen*: **tut** goes second and **weh** jumps to the end.\n\nThe body part is the subject, just like in English:\n\n- one thing → **tut**: *Mein Kopf tut weh.*\n- more than one → **tun**: *Meine Füße tun weh.*\n\nUse **meine** for *die* words and for all plurals: *Meine Hand tut weh. Meine Augen tun weh.*\n\nTo say something doesn't hurt, put **nicht** just before *weh*: *Mein Bauch tut nicht weh.*\n\nYou'll also hear **Mir tut der Kopf weh.** It means the same (literally *To me the head hurts*). You'll learn *mir* later.",
      "table": [
        ["German", "English"],
        ["Mein Kopf tut weh.", "My head hurts."],
        ["Meine Füße tun weh.", "My feet hurt."],
        ["Tut dein Arm weh?", "Does your arm hurt?"],
        ["Wo tut es weh?", "Where does it hurt?"],
        ["Es tut nicht weh.", "It doesn't hurt."]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gesundheit-whats-wrong",
      "title": "Saying what's wrong: sein or haben",
      "body": "Use **sein** for how you *are*: *Ich bin krank. Ich bin müde. Ich bin gesund.*\n\nUse **haben** for things you *have*: *Ich habe Fieber. Ich habe Husten. Ich habe eine Erkältung.*\n\nAches are long compound words: a body part + **Schmerzen** (pains). Say them in the plural with no article: *Ich habe Kopfschmerzen.* English often says *a headache*, but German has no *eine* here.\n\nTwo fixed phrases: the doctor asks **Was fehlt Ihnen?** (What's the matter?), and you say **Gute Besserung!** (Get well soon!) to anyone who's ill.",
      "table": [
        ["English", "German"],
        ["I have a headache.", "Ich habe Kopfschmerzen."],
        ["I have stomach ache.", "Ich habe Bauchschmerzen."],
        ["I have a sore throat.", "Ich habe Halsschmerzen."],
        ["I have back pain.", "Ich habe Rückenschmerzen."],
        ["I have toothache.", "Ich habe Zahnschmerzen."],
        ["I have a temperature.", "Ich habe Fieber."],
        ["I have a cough.", "Ich habe Husten."],
        ["I have a cold.", "Ich habe eine Erkältung."]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gesundheit-sollen",
      "title": "sollen: should, supposed to",
      "body": "**sollen** follows the modal pattern you know from *können, müssen* and *wollen*: no ending for **ich** and **er / sie / es**, and the second verb goes to the end. Its vowel never changes.\n\n**müssen** and **sollen** are close, but not the same:\n\n- **müssen** = it's necessary: *Ich muss arbeiten.* (I have no choice.)\n- **sollen** = someone tells or advises you: *Ich soll im Bett bleiben.* (The doctor says so.)\n\nTo ask for advice, say **Was soll ich machen?** (What should I do?)",
      "table": [
        ["Person", "sollen", "müssen"],
        ["ich", "soll", "muss"],
        ["du", "sollst", "musst"],
        ["er / sie / es", "soll", "muss"],
        ["wir", "sollen", "müssen"],
        ["ihr", "sollt", "müsst"],
        ["sie / Sie", "sollen", "müssen"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gesundheit-imperative",
      "title": "Polite advice: Nehmen Sie …!",
      "body": "To tell someone you call *Sie* what to do, take the *Sie* form of the verb, put it **first**, and keep **Sie** right after it. It ends with an exclamation mark.\n\nSeparable verbs split as usual: *Sie rufen morgen an.* → **Rufen Sie morgen an!**\n\nAdd **bitte** to sound softer: *Bitte bleiben Sie zu Hause.* or *Bleiben Sie bitte zu Hause.*",
      "table": [
        ["Statement", "Advice"],
        ["Sie nehmen die Tabletten.", "Nehmen Sie die Tabletten!"],
        ["Sie bleiben zu Hause.", "Bleiben Sie zu Hause!"],
        ["Sie trinken viel Tee.", "Trinken Sie viel Tee!"],
        ["Sie schlafen viel.", "Schlafen Sie viel!"],
        ["Sie rufen morgen an.", "Rufen Sie morgen an!"]
      ]
    }
  ],
